police in kazakhstan's largest city  almaty tell state media that dozens of  protesters have been killed in what they  call an attempt to storm administrative  buildings and police stations  explosions and apparent gunshots were  also heard in the city's main square  where reporters say hundreds had  gathered to protest against the  government kazakhstan's president has  asked for help and russia will lead an  alliance of former soviet states in  dispatching what they call peacekeeping  forces to help quell the unrest  the protests were triggered by surging  fuel prices  night falls  and the unrest in almaty intensifies  further  what began as a protest against fuel  prices has quickly spiraled out of  control  local media reports that several  security personnel have died  while flights in and out of almaty have  been cancelled  the president has confirmed that  protesters stormed the airport earlier  on wednesday  the protests originally began over the  weekend in the remote oil hub of  zhanojin  but after the government announced an  end to price caps on fuel the unrest  quickly spread to other cities  now a nationwide state of emergency is  in place  in almaty police have been deployed to  curb the unrest  but tear gas and stun grenades have done  little to dissuade the crowds  neither have hundreds of arrests nor the  imposition of a curfew and movement  restrictions in several regions  kazakh president kasim jamaat takayev  said he would reinstate price controls  but by now protesters are calling for  political change beyond the price of  fuel  in a surprise move president takayev  also sacked the government and took  control of the state security committee  ousting former president and long-time  ruler no sultan nazarbayev  now with takayev solely at the helm he  has called in the military and he says  no matter what he will stay in the  capitol  as the head of state and from today the  president of the security council  i intend to act as tough as possible  kazakhstan's authoritarian government  usually tolerates little real opposition  but this time the protesters anger seems  to have caught the authorities off guard  joining me now is journalist bruce pania  a central asia asia specialist he's  following the story for us uh from  prague bruce the internet has been shut  down in kazakhstan now what is the  latest that you're hearing from your  sources on the ground there  well there seemed to be some partial  restoration of the internet um last  night late late uh i don't know if  they've tried to block it out again you  know you mentioned that russia agreed  that they were going to send  some troops over there to help out and  there's there is a what they're calling  in kazakhstan a and counter terrorism  operation underway and alma t at the  moment so i'm sure that the internet is  down in almaty uh and there's probably a  blackout in some of the other other  nearby regions too some of the big  cities as well so um it's it's been hard  to get information for days out there  and until they  come to the point get to the point where  they think they've restored order in  almaty i would imagine it'd be very  difficult to connect  well the size of the protest it does  does come as a surprise really  how could a small uprise against fuel  prices uh get this serious what's the  background to all this  well there's been similar intentions out  there for quite some time i mean there's  a huge disparity in wealth in that  country uh people connected to the  former president nurse nazarbayev and  his family members have done very well i  mean some of them are billionaires they  own you know luxurious properties in  europe for instance in the middle east  uh you know whereas the average person  is getting by on you know a couple  hundred dollars a month and sometimes in  some cases even less than that um  there's there's been problems with  social benefits over the years working  conditions uh you know so there's really  a whole a whole bowl full of problems  that have been that have come up in the  last few years and and you know this  fuel price hike uh just was the catalyst  you know to set off the whole  for everyone to vent their frustrations  for many many years you know and i  should mention also of course that  takayam came in after nazarbayev  resigned in march 2019  there were expectations on the part of  many people that this was going to be a  new era in kazakhstan but  you know it never it never materialized  uh the government continued to operate  exactly as it did there were no  opposition parties registered uh no real  reforms were implemented in the country  um you know so so people just like i say  got frustrated that after nearly three  years really nothing had changed except  the who was the president of the country  well the so-called collective security  treaty organization will send  peacekeepers now to kazakhstan  at the country's request does that mean  that russian troops will crack down on  the protesters  well it's it's interesting crackdown  might not be exactly the right word  kazakh forces seem to be already working  and you know i want to make clear too  that's as far as i've been able to tell  this is only happening in almaty  kazakhstan's a very big country  uh you know so but um you know the  appearance of russian forces on the  streets of almaty will bring back  horrible memories from the soviet air  without a doubt but it might be enough  to restore help restore calm  in that area but there's a lot of  problems they're going to face for this  in the future uh you know people in  kazakhstan will not be happy that takaya  have had to resort to calling in you  know the csto to help him out um you  know and he's he's framed this late all  of a sudden last night as being the work  of a band of terrorists who were trained  outside the country and there was  absolutely no evidence that that's the  case this appears to be purely a popular  uprising uh you know it's sort of  ridiculous if you look at the pictures  some of these protesters they're oil  workers you know women people like that  how these got to be banned of terrorists  who were trained outside the country i  have no idea but he used that as the  pretext to call in the cso he's going to  pay a price for that for you know the  days months years to come probably  journalists bruce palia there thank you  very much  thank you  you
 

